WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.415
Voltage doubles. Current always doubles?

00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:07.945
Voltage pushes, resistance holds back, current flows.

00:00:08.035 --> 00:00:11.705
Two volts, ten ohms: point two amperes.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:17.472
Now four volts, same resistor. More current, less, or the same?

00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:23.452
More: point four. Twice the push, twice the flow.

00:00:23.872 --> 00:00:26.412
Why? Only the voltage changed.

00:00:26.502 --> 00:00:33.428
Voltage over current stays ten ohms. Ohm's law: voltage equals current times resistance.

00:00:34.148 --> 00:00:37.058
Always? Only at constant temperature.

00:00:37.198 --> 00:00:41.338
A hot bulb? Resistance rises, current won't double.

00:00:41.728 --> 00:00:46.505
Your turn: same resistor, six volts. What does the meter read?

00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:53.182
Point six amperes. If you got that, the rule is yours.

00:00:53.572 --> 00:00:56.099
Try the free lesson on Ohm's law.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.359
Professor Diki Simplified.
